\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The continuum integrability condition}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Equation (3.17) of \citef{lasenby1998gravity} is the continuum
condition
##{\nabla\wedge\bar{\mathsf h}^{-1}(a)=0.}
The equation is a citation. This note does not prove it. On one
fixed finite complex, \ref{fdip-00OE} replaces that condition by
the vanishing of the cohomology class of the translation cochain.
A gauge with rotational component #{1} assigns a vector #{X_x} to
each vertex and changes the cochain by the coboundary of #{X}. The
assignment relabels positions on the cells already present. It does
not supply a continuum coordinate on that complex.}

\p{The reference cochain of \ref{fdip-00OF} fails to be pure gauge
exactly when some period #{N\epsilon_i} is nonzero in #{V}. In
that case no single-valued vertex cochain reproduces the reference
frame. The displacement links of the cited paper are pairs
#{(R,a)} for which #{\alpha(R)=R} and #{R\tilde{R}=1}, so the
translation equals #{Rc\tilde{R}}. Their quadratic form has rank
#{4} and is not a positive form. A positive form of that rank is a
separate case. The argument above uses neither sign pattern nor the
order on #{\mathbb{R}}.}
